  "cli_result_text": "Submitted the manifest files that establish the dependency edges:\n\n- **`next.js--v13.4.12/packages/next/package.json`** \u2014 declares `@babel/core@7.18.0`, `@babel/parser` (via root resolutions pin `7.18.0`), `@babel/traverse@7.18.0`, `@babel/types@7.18.0`, `@babel/generator@7.18.0`, plus multiple `@babel/plugin-*`/`@babel/preset-*` packages. This is the real edge: Next.js bundles a Babel-based transform pipeline (used as fallback/for custom `.babelrc` configs alongside SWC), so a new `DecoratorMetadata` AST node reaches Next.js's traverse/codegen paths.\n- **`next.js--v13.4.12/package.json`** (workspace root) \u2014 the `resolutions` block pins `@babel/core`, `@babel/parser`, `@babel/types`, `@babel/traverse` to `7.18.0`/`7.18.0`, corroborating the edge and showing the exact version Next.js consumes.\n- **`webpack--v5.88.2/package.json`** \u2014 the counter-evidence: `@babel/core` and `@babel/preset-react` appear **only** under `devDependencies` (used for webpack's own test fixtures/tooling), not in `dependencies`. Webpack parses JS with `acorn` (listed in `dependencies`), not Babel \u2014 so the parser AST change does **not** reach webpack's own source; it would only matter transitively if a project pairs webpack with `babel-loader`, which isn't vendored in this repo.",
